Q: What is this whole "publication safety" hoopla and in particular Point <4> from above?
A: It has to do with final fields in an object. They are initialized and read subsequently 
   without holding locks. So how does one make sure they are done correctly.
   Refer tutorials/arch/whyloadbarriers for more details of what can go wrong absent
   LoadBarriers.

Q: In simple words what must the compiler do?
A: On the producer side viz., constructor if even a single final field store took place
   emit #StoreStore at the end of constructor.

   On the receiver side .. well thats something you should refer to the above document for ..
   beyond scope of this document.

   For x86 and SPARC TSO nothing needs to be done for the processor reordering part (DaveD has
   confirmed the same too)

   PowerPC and ARM have weak memory models too but none dare reorder dependent loads like Alpha
   so reader side is safe. Producer side meaning end of constructor yes you ll have to add
   a #StoreStore

   For Alpha DEC yes do as Sanjay says either place a loadload barrier before every final field
   load or do something clever like global memory barrier and batch it with your GC... 
